First, we will create a context to manage the state of the tooltip.

```jsx
import React, { createContext, useState } from 'react';

export const TooltipContext = createContext();

export const TooltipProvider = ({ children }) => {
  const [tooltip, setTooltip] = useState(null);

  const closeTooltip = () => {
    setTooltip(null);
  };

  const openTooltip = (content, position) => {
    setTooltip({ content, position });
  };

  return (
    <TooltipContext.Provider value={{ openTooltip, closeTooltip, tooltip }}>
      {children}
    </TooltipContext.Provider>
  );
};
```

Now, we will create the Tooltip component that uses the context to open and close the tooltip.

```jsx
import React, { useContext, useState } from 'react';
import { Tooltip as ReactTooltip } from 'react-tooltip';
import 'react-tooltip/dist/react-tooltip.css';
import { TooltipContext } from './TooltipContext';

const Tooltip = ({ children, tooltipId }) => {
  const { tooltip, openTooltip, closeTooltip } = useContext(TooltipContext);

  const handleMouseEnter = () => {
    if (!tooltip) openTooltip(children, tooltipId);
  };

  const handleMouseLeave = () => {
    if (tooltip && tooltip.id === tooltipId) closeTooltip();
  };

  return (
    <>
      {React.cloneElement(children, {
        onMouseEnter: handleMouseEnter,
        onMouseLeave: handleMouseLeave,
      })}
      {tooltip && tooltip.id === tooltipId && (
        <ReactTooltip id={tooltipId} effect="solid">
          {tooltip.content}
        </ReactTooltip>
      )}
    </>
  );
};

export default Tooltip;
```

Finally, we will use the Tooltip component in our NextJS application.

```jsx
import Tooltip from '../components/Tooltip';

const MyComponent = () => {
  return (
    <Tooltip tooltipId="my-tooltip">
      <button>Hover me</button>
    </Tooltip>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;
```

Now, when you hover over the button, the tooltip will open, and when you move the mouse away, it will close. If you have multiple Tooltip components on the same page, they will behave independently, closing the previously opened tooltip when a new one is opened.

