Here's an example of an onSubmit function that sends form data to a GraphQL API using Apollo Client in a React component:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { useMutation, gql } from '@apollo/client';
import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form';
import { useHistory } from 'react-router-dom';

const CREATE_DATA_ENTRY = gql`
  mutation CreateDataEntry($input: CreateDataEntryInput!) {
    createDataEntry(input: $input) {
      id
      title
      description
      createdAt
    }
  }
`;

const DataEntryForm = () => {
  const history = useHistory();
  const { register, handleSubmit } = useForm();
  const [createDataEntry, { loading }] = useMutation(CREATE_DATA_ENTRY);

  const onSubmit = async (data) => {
    try {
      const { title, description } = data;
      const variables = { input: { title, description } };
      const { data: { createDataEntry } } = await createDataEntry(variables);
      history.push(`/data-entry/${createDataEntry.id}`);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
    }
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>
      <input type="text" placeholder="Title" ref={register} />
      <textarea placeholder="Description" ref={register} />
      <button type="submit" disabled={loading}>
        {loading ? 'Submitting...' : 'Submit'}
      </button>
    </form>
  );
};

export default DataEntryForm;
```

In this example, we're using the `useMutation` hook from Apollo Client to create a mutation for creating a data entry. We define the GraphQL mutation using the `gql` tagged template literal.

The `DataEntryForm` component uses the `useForm` hook from `react-hook-form` to manage the form state. We define the `onSubmit` function that will be called when the form is submitted. Inside the `onSubmit` function, we call the `createDataEntry` mutation with the form data as variables. If the mutation is successful, we navigate to the data entry details page using the `useHistory` hook from `react-router-dom`.

